     1	"""Declaration-update orchestration — plan → apply → recover (libs-handoff S2).
     2	
     3	The one place that owns the 8-step declaration-update protocol clients used to
     4	re-assemble by hand (parse, resolve, head-capture, project, diff, open the
     5	right canonical store with signing, CAS absorb, reconcile the ``.vertex``
     6	file). LIBS_CHANGES P0.2 ("one declaration-update orchestration API") and
     7	P0.3 ("recoverable file/store declaration synchronization").
     8	
     9	Shape: free functions, like :func:`engine.handle.open_vertex` — a ceremony is
    10	a one-shot operation keyed on a vertex path, not a held session, so it does
    11	NOT live on :class:`~engine.handle.VertexHandle` (which serializes a live
    12	read/receive stream). Signing rides the ratified S3
    13	:class:`~engine.handle.CredentialProvider` shape — the import DAG forbids
    14	engine→custody, so key material is always supplied by the caller,
    15	operation-fresh.
    16	
    17	Protocol rules owned here
    18	-------------------------
    19	
    20	- **Store-first after genesis.** The store-backed declaration is the
    21	  authority; the ``.vertex`` file is an ingress/presentation cache. Apply
    22	  order is: durable intent → store ceremony (atomic, CAS-guarded by the S1b
    23	  machinery) → atomic file replace → intent removal. Every prefix of that
    24	  sequence is recoverable.
    25	- **Durable intent** (P0.3) lives as a SIBLING of the ``.vertex`` file —
    26	  ``<name>.vertex.intent`` — written atomically (tempfile + fsync + rename)
    27	  BEFORE anything mutates. Sibling, not store-adjacent, because (a) the
    28	  vertex path is the one handle plan, apply, and recover all share; (b) a
    29	  JSONL-canonical store has TWO adjacent artifacts (log + index) and no
    30	  single obvious sibling slot; and (c) the store locator is exactly the kind
    31	  of thing a ceremony may be mid-edit on — the file being reconciled is the
    32	  stable coordinate. It survives process death and is discoverable by
    33	  anything that knows the vertex (``intent_path_for``).
    34	- **Pending intent blocks new ceremonies.** ``plan``/``apply`` refuse (typed)
    35	  while an intent file exists — run :func:`recover_declaration_update`
    36	  first. This is what makes the ``conflict`` classification non-lossy:
    37	  nothing overwrites the evidence.
    38	- **Staleness is the store's CAS, not a re-check race.** ``apply`` passes the
    39	  preview's captured head to ``absorb_edit(expected_head=…)``; a moved head
    40	  refuses inside the store's own transaction
    41	  (:class:`~engine.sqlite_store.StaleDeclarationHead`) with the log
    42	  byte-identical — reused as the ``"stale"`` result, never re-spelled here.
    43	  Genesis mode has no CAS parameter; its stale signal is
    44	  :class:`~engine.sqlite_store.GenesisExists` (a concurrent absorb opened
    45	  the lineage first).
    46	- **Recovery never guesses.** :func:`recover_declaration_update` classifies
    47	  by comparing the store's CURRENT resolved projection fingerprint against
    48	  the fingerprints pinned in the intent: proposed → ``already-applied`` /
    49	  ``safe-to-finish``; the captured pre-ceremony state → ``not-applied``
    50	  (store untouched — the process died before the store commit; the intent is
    51	  void); anything else → ``conflict`` (another writer landed; intent and
    52	  file are left exactly as found). ``not-applied`` is an honest EXTENSION of
    53	  the task's three classes: store-first ordering makes "intent written,
    54	  store never touched" a reachable state, and folding it into either
    55	  ``conflict`` or ``safe-to-finish`` would be a guess.
    56	- **Fingerprints are order-independent.** Documents are sorted by
    57	  ``(kind, subject)`` before hashing: the file projects documents in
